WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:14.000
[Narrator] The interior of our bodies is hidden to&nbsp;
us. What happens beneath the skin is mysterious,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:20.640
fearful, amazing. In antiquity, the body's&nbsp;
internal structure was the subject of speculation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:27.200
fantasy and some study but there were few efforts&nbsp;
to represent it in pictures. The invention of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:32.640
printing press in the 15th century and the&nbsp;
cascade of print technologies that followed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:39.040
helped to inspire a new spectacular science of&nbsp;
anatomy and new spectacular visions of the body.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:47.760
Anatomical imagery proliferated, detailed and&nbsp;
informative but also whimsical, surreal beautiful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:54.480
and grotesque a dream anatomy that reveals as much&nbsp;
about the outer world as it does the inner self.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.080
Over the centuries anatomy has become&nbsp;
a visual vocabulary of realism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:04.000
We regard the anatomical&nbsp;
body as our inner reality;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:11.680
a medium through which we imagine society,&nbsp;
culture and the human condition. Drawn principally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:16.960
from the collections of the National Library of&nbsp;
Medicine, Dream Anatomy shows off the anatomical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:25.840
imagination in some of its most astonishing&nbsp;
incarnations, from 1500 to the present.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.760
With the founding of the first&nbsp;
medical schools in medieval Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:41.520
anatomy ascended to a prominent&nbsp;
position in the medical curriculum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.440
Human dissection was performed as a ritual&nbsp;
that illustrated the treatises of revered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:52.240
ancient authors and that dramatized the power&nbsp;
and knowledge of the medical profession.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:57.600
The cutting open of the body was not&nbsp;
a scientific study of the cadaver&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:03.520
but a performance designed to illustrate the&nbsp;
anatomical treatises of Galen, the great anatomist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:11.280
of antiquity. [Sappol] Galen was an iconic figure&nbsp;
for people in the middle ages. He represented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:20.000
the sum of learned authority on the human&nbsp;
body. [Narrator] But the old anatomies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.560
were primarily based on the dissection of&nbsp;
animals and filled with numerous errors.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.640
The mid 15th century invention of the printing&nbsp;
press and the rise of a new spirit of critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:38.400
inquiry, associated with the renaissance,&nbsp;
inspired a scientific revolution in anatomy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.680
Anatomists began to dissect in order to&nbsp;
investigate the structure of the body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:47.840
and produced texts illustrated with&nbsp;
images based on their dissections.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:55.520
Published in 1543, the Humani&nbsp;
Corporis Fabrica by Andreas Vesalius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:01.360
was the founding text of modern anatomy.&nbsp;
The Fabrica set a new standard for accuracy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:05.600
and a new standard for the artistry of&nbsp;
scientific illustration. [Sappol] It's part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:11.600
of a larger intellectual movement in society&nbsp;
in which people are rethinking ancient wisdom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.280
and creating new wisdom and it's not enough&nbsp;
though to make a direct empirical observation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.160
of the body and criticize the ancient text.&nbsp;
Vesalius also says we have to make new texts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.360
and these new texts, Vesalius argues, cannot&nbsp;
just be written descriptions of the human body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:33.200
they need to be illustrated texts and they have&nbsp;
to show something of what the anatomist sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:38.560
and that is the Vesalian revolution in anatomy&nbsp;
and it created the modern science of anatomy.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:46.960
[Narrator] In the early modern era, 1450 to&nbsp;
1750, the boundary between art and science&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:53.280
was ill-defined. Anatomists and their artist&nbsp;
collaborators made use of familiar modes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:04:01.280
representation, the iconography of landscape,&nbsp;
nudity, mythology and christianity. Artists tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:07.760
to create illustrations that were accurate but&nbsp;
also amazing, beautiful and entertaining. [Sappol]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:13.120
In the early modern period science meant any&nbsp;
organized body of knowledge so you could have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:19.760
the science of theology or the science of law. Art&nbsp;
meant any work from the human hand that involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:24.560
skill. People often talked about the art&nbsp;
of dissection or the art of medicine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:31.440
The presumption is that a successful scientific&nbsp;
illustration would also have to be an artful

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:41.840
illustration.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:51.120
Between 1680 and 1800, anatomists began purging&nbsp;
imaginative elements from scientific illustration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:57.200
The truth value of anatomy, they argued,&nbsp;
was compromised by visual metaphors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:04.800
fantastic landscapes and comic poses. Ultimately,&nbsp;
two styles of anatomical realism emerged,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:10.960
one aimed to show the reality of dissection, the&nbsp;
cutting open of a particular body with all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:17.120
prosthetics furniture and setting of dissection&nbsp;
and the ugliness of anatomical mutilation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.120
The other aimed to show a higher reality,&nbsp;
displaying beautified, cleaned up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:29.200
idealized bodies and body parts that float in&nbsp;
air with no reference to any one dissection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:37.120
As old print technologies were perfected and new&nbsp;
ones invented, anatomical illustration began to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:42.640
achieve greater technical precision and a&nbsp;
brilliant and dreamlike hyper aestheticism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:48.560
that showed off with great artistry a more&nbsp;
sophisticated knowledge and heightened perception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:54.240
of the boundaries and surfaces of the body.&nbsp;
[Sappol] The competition among anatomists for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.240 --> 00:06:03.200
most elaborate printed anatomical atlases means&nbsp;
that they're searching for the best printers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:08.480
the best engravers; the best artists that they&nbsp;
can get at the same time there's a competition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.480 --> 00:06:14.160
among printers for patronage and the anatomical&nbsp;
illustration for them becomes an exemplary work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.840
it helps foster the development of improved&nbsp;
technology so there's a synergistic effect.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:31.520
[Narrator]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:38.160
By 1800 anatomy had been defined as a science,&nbsp;
the investigation of the real, rather than an art.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:46.000
Anatomy could no longer tolerate humor fancy and&nbsp;
ornament yet fantastic images of the anatomical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:52.480
body continued to proliferate. Cast out of&nbsp;
medical illustration, imaginative anatomy found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:57.600
new homes in academic art where anatomy&nbsp;
was an important part of the curriculum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:02.720
in popular health where the need to&nbsp;
appeal to the public and a free hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:08.960
inspired the creation of vivid playful and&nbsp;
eccentric images and in political cartoons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:15.520
film and fiction. Wherever anatomical images&nbsp;
could be made to serve. [Sappol] In our society,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:22.320
anatomy is seen as an authoritative source for&nbsp;
views of the body, renderings of the body. We have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:31.280
faith in anatomical imaging that that&nbsp;
expresses the reality of who we are and that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.400
a resource for artists who also want to in&nbsp;
a different way convey the reality of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:42.000
human body and the reality of human existence.&nbsp;
[Narrator] Photography, radiography, digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:47.360
imaging and computer modeling have multiplied&nbsp;
the possibilities for manipulation and play.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:54.640
Artists and scientists are again exploring and&nbsp;
re-imagining the body and investigating the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:08:06.560
boundaries between their respective disciplines.&nbsp;
We continue to dream the anatomical body.

